hadiths[50]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1376","Sayyidna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Once he prayed (after finishing his prayer) he said, “Satan came before me to spoil my prayer, so Allah overpowered me. Dua remembered: \O my Lord: grant me a kingdom that no one else will get after me.\"" (The Holy Prophet"," peace and blessings be upon him) says"," So Allah humiliated this devil and sent him back."""
"1377","Sayyidina Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Wudu is the key to prayer. Imam Abu Hanifah, may God have mercy on him, says: This means reciting tashahhud (i.e. recite tashahhud after every two rak'ahs)."
"1378","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us the Zuhr prayer or perhaps the Asr prayer. Yes, the prayer has been shortened, Sayyidna Abu Bakr and Sayyidna Umar were also present, but they did not have the courage to address the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \Neither have I forgotten"," nor has the prayer been shortened.\"" He said"," \""O Messenger of Allah"," perhaps you have forgotten.\"" So the people indicated yes: The Prophet returned to his place and after offering the remaining two rak'ats"," he returned the salam"," then he prostrated like the normal prostrations or a little longer than that while saying the Takbeer"," then he raised his head and said the Takbeer. A narrator named Muhammad was asked: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greet before prostrating himself? So he replied"," \""I do not remember these words regarding Sayyidna Abu Huraira"," but I have been told this: Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain"," may Allah be pleased with him"," has narrated this: Then the Prophet"," may God bless him and grant him peace"," greeted him."""
"1379","This tradition is narrated along with some other isnads, but it does not contain these words: So people pointed out, these words were narrated only by the narrator named Hamad bin Zayd."
"1380","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was leading the people in prayer, some donkeys passed in front of him, and Ayyash bin Abu Rabiyyah, one of those who were praying in the lead of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), began to say Subhan Allah. He said, O Messenger of Allah, I said: Yes, because I have heard that if a donkey passes in front, the prayer breaks."
"1381","Salem bin Abdullah narrates the statement of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar RA): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Sayyidna Abu Bakr and Sayyidna Umar RA said: Nothing breaks the prayer of a Muslim, but you try to stop him as much as possible (so that he does not pass in front of you during the prayer)."
"1382","Sayyiduna Abu Saeed al-Khudri (RA) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW): Nothing breaks the prayer (i.e. it does not break the prayer by passing in front of it)."
"1383","Sayyidina Abu Umamah narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Nothing breaks the prayer."
"1384","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) says: It is said: Nothing breaks a Muslim's prayer (i.e., prayer is not broken due to the passage of anything in front of it)."
"1385","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this statement of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A dog, a woman, or a donkey does not break a man's prayer (by passing in front of him), but prevent them from passing in front of you as much as you can."
"1386","Sayyidna Fazl bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) came to meet Sayyidna Abbas (RA) in his neighborhood, where the Prophet (PBUH) offered the Asr prayer, a small dog and a donkey passed in front of him, but they were not rebuked and they were not reprimanded."
"1387","Sayyidna Fazl bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) came to meet Sayyidna Abbas (RA)."
"1388","Sayyiduna Fazl bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) came to us and we were in our residence. The Prophet (PBUH) led us in the Asr prayer. A small dog and a donkey passed in front of him, which were our (pets), but we did not stop them and did not return them."
"1389","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: Once I was discussing with Sayyidna Umar Bin Khattab (RA) about a problem of prayer. At the same time, Sayyidna Abd al-Rahman bin Awf (RA) also came to us and said, “Shall I narrate to you the hadith that I heard from the Prophet (PBUH)?” We said: “Yes.” If there is a doubt about it, he should pray so much that the doubt becomes about the increase."
"1390","Makhool narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: When a person is in doubt about his prayer and he does not know whether he has prayed more (rak'ats) or less, then if the doubt is about one rak'at or two rak'ats, he should consider it as one, if it is about two or three, then two, if it is three or four, then consider it as three until he becomes delusional. be about This same tradition is narrated from Sayyidna Abd al-Rahman bin Awf on the authority of Sayyidna Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) along with another chain of transmission."
"1391","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidna Abd al-Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him): Whoever doubts whether a prayer should be three or four rak'ahs should complete the prayer (i.e. perform one more rak'ah) because an increase in it is better than a decrease in it."
"1392","Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Bazaz, Abu Bakr narrated to us, he narrated to us from Ja’far ibn Muhammad ibn Fudhil, he narrated from Ammar ibn Matar al-Anbari (who used to come in spirit), he narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban, he narrated from his father, he from Makhool, he narrated from Karib (slave of Ibn Abbas), he narrated from Ibn Abbas, he narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may God be pleased with him, that the Messenger of God said: Allah, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \If a person neglects three or four (rak'ahs) in prayer"," he should complete the prayer"," because doing more is better than doing less.\"""""
"1393","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidna Abd al-Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When a person makes a mistake about two or one rak'ah, he should consider it as one, and when he is in doubt about two rak'ahs, he should consider them two, and when he is in doubt about three or four, he should consider them as three, and then complete the remaining prayer, until he becomes more delusional. Be it not about lack, then when he is sitting (i.e. reciting tashahhud) he should perform two prostrations (i.e. perform prostration)."
"1394","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this hadith regarding the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): On the day Sayyiduna Dhuwalideen came to him (i.e. the day he told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prostrating himself in prayer), on that day the Prophet (peace be upon him) performed two prostrations of prostration after returning the salutation. The word of these two traditions is the same."
"1395","Sayyidna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) prostrated after saluting on the day of Sayyidna Dhu-Walideen (the Day of Attention)."
"1396","Sayyiduna Abu Saeed al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When a person does not know how many rak'ahs he has performed, he should pray three or four rak'ahs, add one more rak'ah, and then perform two sajdahs of Sahu while he is sitting (i.e. reciting tashahhud). If he pays, these two will disgrace the devil."
"1397","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates the saying of the Prophet (PBUH): When a person doubts during prayer whether he has performed three rak'ahs or four rak'ahs, then he should perform one more rak'ah until he is sure that the doubt is greater, then he should prostrate before returning the salam. If so, both of these will be a source of humiliation for Satan."
"1398","Sayyidina Abu Saeed al-Khudri (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When a person has a doubt during the prayer and does not know how many rak'ahs he has performed, three or four, he should put the doubt aside and base it on what he believes in. Both of these (sajdahs) will be disgraceful to Shaytaan if the rakats are performed."
"1399","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said this: When a person has a doubt about his prayer, he should put the doubt aside and base it on faith. Through this, his prayer will be completed and the two prostrations of Sahu will dust the nose of Satan."
"1400","Sayyiduna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW): When a person is in doubt about his prayer and does not know how many rak'ahs he has performed: four yatin? So he should put his doubt aside and base it on faith, then stand up and pray one rak'at, then when he is sitting (i.e. reciting the tashahhud) he should perform prostration twice before turning the salam. Both prostrations will make Satan's nose dusty."
"1401","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: When a person is in doubt about his prayer, if he is sure that he has performed three rak'ahs, then he should perform one more rak'ah and perform two prostrations. He had performed three rak'ahs, now the rak'ah he has performed will become the fourth rak'ah, and the two prostrations of Sajdah Sahu will dust the nose of the devil, and he had previously performed four rak'ahs, so now with what he has performed, it will become the fifth, and these two sajdahs will make the fifth rak'ah equal."
"1402","Abd al-Muhaiman narrates the statement of his grandfather Munzeer bin Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding his father: These companions are among the Naqba of Banus Sa’idah."
"1403","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said to us, \When a person is praying and he does not know whether he has performed more Rak'ats or less?\"" So he should prostrate twice while he is sitting (i.e. do it during tashahhud)"," then after that he should return the salam."""
"1404","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When the muezzin gives the call to prayer, the devil leaves the mosque, his air is emitted. creates various thoughts in the mind, after which he does not remember whether he has increased or decreased the prayer. When a person is faced with this situation, when he is sitting (i.e. while reciting the tashahhud), he should perform Sajdah twice."
"1405","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Whoever is in doubt about his prayer and is not sure whether he has performed three rak'ahs or four rak'ahs? So he should get up and perform another rak'ah and then after that, when he is sitting, he should prostrate twice before returning the salam."
"1406","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said that when a person prays and does not know whether he has performed three rak'ahs or four, then he should complete it until he is sure that he has performed the complete prayer, then after that he should prostrate twice before turning the salutation. If already married, these prostrations will become a disgrace to Satan."
"1407","Muhammad bin Yusuf narrates: Me?? He heard his father narrate that once Sayyiduna Mu'awiyah led these people in prayer, they prayed two rak'ahs and then stood up even though they were required to sit. The people said SubhanAllah upon that, but they did not sit until they sat down to return the salutation (in the final tashahhud) and they prostrated twice. These are the words of a narrator named Neshapuri: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing the same."
"1408","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered a prayer. The narrator of this tradition, Ibrahim, narrates: I do not know whether the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned any increase or decrease in this prayer. (Sayyidna Abdullah Radiyallahu Anhu narrates:) When the Holy Prophet (Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam) greeted him, he was asked: O Messenger of Allah, has there been any new order regarding prayer? The Prophet (PBUH) said no, why what happened? The people said, \You have prayed like this.\"" The Prophet"," peace and blessings of God be upon him"," straightened his legs"," turned his face towards the Qibla"," and prostrated himself twice. If he is in doubt about his prayer"," he should try to find the correct form"," then complete the prayer according to the same calculation"," then offer salutations and after that do Sajdah twice."""
"1409","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that when a person is in doubt about his prayer, he should try to adopt the correct posture and then perform Sajdah twice."
"1410","Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am a human being and I forget, just like you people forget. If a person is in doubt about his prayer, he should examine which form of prayer is closest to the correctness of the prayer, then complete the prayer according to the same calculation and then perform Sajdah twice."
"1411","Al-Qamah narrates: Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud (RA) prostrated twice after returning the salam, and he said: The Prophet (PBUH) also performed these two prostrations after returning the salam."
"1412","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Bahina (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the Zuhr prayer. He stood up after praying two rak'ahs."
"1413","Salem bin Abdullah, on the authority of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, on the authority of Sayyidna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: The person who follows the Imam (due to some mistake of his own), it is not necessary to perform prostration on him, but if a mistake is made by the Imam, it is necessary to perform prostration on the Imam, and it is also necessary for those who lead the prayer. But if there is a mistake on the part of the person performing the prayer following the lead of the imam, then prostration is not required on him, rather the imam is sufficient for him."
"1414","Salem bin Abdullah, on the authority of his father, narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet, peace and blessings of God be upon him: Prostration on any mistake is obligatory only when a person stands instead of sitting or sits instead of standing."
"1415","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: Once Sayyidna Umar (RA) was discussing with me about increasing in prayer, while Sayyidna (RA) Abd al-Rahman bin Awf (RA) came to us and stayed with us."
"1416","Narrated Ubaidullah bin Abdullah (this statement of Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas: Once I was with Sayyiduna Umar, we were discussing about prayer. Meanwhile, Sayyiduna Abd al-Rahman bin Awf came and said, “Should I not tell you the hadith that I heard from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace?” I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying that when you are in doubt about a lack during the prayer, continue to pray until the doubt becomes about an increase."
"1417","Abu Ubaydah bin Abdullah narrates the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud, may Allah be pleased with him) that when you are in the state of prayer and you are in doubt about three or four rak'ahs, and you think that four have been recited, then after reciting the tashahhud, prostrate twice, do this while you are sitting and before turning the salam, then recite the tashahud again and then the salam. turn Some other narrators have narrated it, but they have not narrated it as a marfu' hadeeth."
"1418","Sayyidna Mugheera bin Shuba (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When the imam stands up (instead of sitting) after performing two rak'ahs, and remembers this before standing up, he should sit down. Other narrators have narrated it in the same way."
"1419","Sayyidna Mugheera bin Shuba (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When a person has a doubt and he stands up (instead of sitting) after praying the rakat, if he stands fully, he should continue praying in the same way and then perform Sajdah twice."
"1420","Some narrators of this tradition have been criticized."
"1421","Muhammad bin Hanafiyah narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that ablution is the key to prayer. A narrator named Ubaidullah has quoted some words differently."
"1422","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: When the imam is sitting in tashahhud after reciting the last rak'at, and following his example, someone breaks his ablution before the imam turns his salutations, then that person's prayer will be counted as complete. The narrator of this hadith is Abd al-Rahman bin Ziyad Zaeef, it has not been recognized as authentic."
"1423","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: When the imam finishes the prayer and he is sitting while he is sitting, if his ablution is broken before making the salam, then his prayer will be complete and the person who is following him will complete his prayer."
"1424","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the imam breaks his ablution after rising from the last prostration and sitting upright, his prayer will be counted as complete, and the prayer of the person who followed him from the beginning of the prayer will also be counted as complete."
"1425","Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I had a complaint of piles and I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it."
